  weatherSection={{
    heading: "Florence's Tennessee River Location Creates Unique Cleaning Demands",
    body: `
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">Florence sits on the south bank of the Tennessee River, and that geography defines its weather reality. The river moderates temperature extremes somewhat but contributes significantly to year-round humidity — Florence's average relative humidity stays above 70% for most of the year, peaking in summer months when it regularly exceeds 85%. This sustained humidity is the primary driver of mold growth in Florence's older housing stock, particularly in the crawlspace-foundation homes that dominate the historic residential areas west of downtown.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">Flooding is a genuine and recurring risk in Florence. The Tennessee River has flood stages that affect the riverfront district and low-lying areas near Wilson Dam. Severe thunderstorms — which arrive with particular violence from the west across open terrain — produce flash flooding in streets and drainage systems throughout the city. We have responded to flood-related water damage calls in Florence more times than we can count, and our familiarity with the city's drainage patterns helps us assess scope quickly when we arrive.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">The Shoals region also sits in a high-pollen zone. Florence's proximity to the Tennessee River bottomlands, the agricultural land south of the city, and the hardwood forests of the Appalachian foothills to the east creates a pollen environment that rivals any in the state. Spring tree pollen, summer grass pollen, and fall ragweed keep allergen loads high in Florence homes from March through October. Families with respiratory sensitivities in Florence call us for seasonal carpet and upholstery cleaning as consistently as any service we provide.</p>
      <p>Winter ice and freeze events affect Florence with less frequency than areas further north and east, but when a significant cold event hits the Shoals — as happened in February 2021 — the city's older housing stock, with supply lines in exterior walls that were built before modern insulation standards, is vulnerable. We handled multiple burst pipe emergency calls in Florence's historic neighborhoods during that event.</p>
    `,
